{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}User Guide – Subnets & IP Management{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
{% set section_title = 'Subnets & IP Management' %}
{{ include('user_guide/_breadcrumb.html.twig') }}

<div class="d-flex justify-content-between align-items-center mb-4">
    <h1 class="h3 mb-0"><i class="bi bi-diagram-2 me-2"></i>Subnets &amp; IP Management</h1>
    <a href="{{ path('subnet_index') }}" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-primary">
        <i class="bi bi-arrow-right-circle me-1"></i>Go to Subnets
    </a>
</div>

<div class="row g-4">
<div class="col-lg-9">

<div class="card mb-4" id="overview">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-info-circle me-1"></i>Overview</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>A <strong>subnet</strong> defines a range of IP addresses on your network (e.g. <code>10.1.2.0/24</code>). DashDDI tracks which addresses in each subnet are allocated to host interfaces, which are reserved, and which are free.</p>
        <p class="mb-0">Subnets can hold both IPv4 and IPv6 CIDR blocks simultaneously. When a network interface is assigned to a subnet, its IP address is drawn from that subnet's address space.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="subnet-list">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-list-ul me-1"></i>Subnet List Views</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>The Subnets page (<strong>DHCP → Subnets</strong>) offers three view tabs:</p>
        <dl class="row mb-0">
            <dt class="col-sm-3">By Name</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Alphabetical list of all subnets. Fastest for finding a subnet by label.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">IPv4 Tree</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Subnets grouped and indented by CIDR containment — useful for understanding how your address space is structured.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">IPv6 Tree</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Same as IPv4 tree but for IPv6 address space.</dd>
        </dl>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="create-subnet">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-plus-circle me-1"></i>Creating a Subnet</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <dl class="row mb-0">
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Name <span class="text-danger">*</span></dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">A human-readable label, e.g. <code>Server Room A</code> or <code>Student WiFi</code>.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">IPv4 CIDR</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">The IPv4 address range in CIDR notation, e.g. <code>10.1.2.0/24</code>. Leave blank for IPv6-only subnets.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">IPv6 CIDR</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">The IPv6 address range, e.g. <code>2001:db8:1::/48</code>. Leave blank for IPv4-only subnets.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Gateway</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">The default gateway IP address for this subnet (used in DHCP config generation).</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">VLAN</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">The VLAN ID (1–4094) associated with this subnet. Informational only — DashDDI does not configure switch VLANs.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">VRF</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">The Virtual Routing and Forwarding instance this subnet belongs to, if your network uses VRFs for traffic isolation.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">DNS Views</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Associate this subnet with one or more DNS views. The reverse zone for this subnet will be generated for the selected views only.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">DNSSEC Policy</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Apply a DNSSEC policy for inline zone signing of records in this subnet.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Is Container</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Mark this subnet as a supernet (container) that holds smaller subnets. Container subnets don't have individual IP allocations and don't appear in DHCP config.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Lease Retention</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">How long (in days) to keep soft-deleted hosts and interfaces from this subnet before permanently purging them. Overrides the application-wide default.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Description</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Free-text notes shown on the subnet detail page.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Tags</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Labels for grouping and filtering subnets.</dd>
        </dl>

        <h6 class="mt-4">Reverse Zone SOA</h6>
        <p>These fields populate the SOA (Start of Authority) record for this subnet's automatically generated reverse DNS zone (e.g. <code>1.168.192.in-addr.arpa</code>). All fields are optional — leave them blank to omit the reverse SOA from the generated zone file.</p>
        <dl class="row mb-0">
            <dt class="col-sm-3">SOA Nameserver</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">The primary nameserver for the reverse zone, e.g. <code>ns1.example.com.</code> (include the trailing dot).</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">SOA Email</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">The zone administrator email in DNS format, e.g. <code>hostmaster.example.com.</code> (replace the <code>@</code> with a dot and include the trailing dot).</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">SOA Refresh</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">How often secondary nameservers check for updates, in seconds (e.g. <code>3600</code>).</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">SOA Retry</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">How long a secondary waits before retrying a failed refresh, in seconds (e.g. <code>900</code>).</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">SOA Expire</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">How long a secondary continues serving the zone if the primary is unreachable, in seconds (e.g. <code>604800</code>).</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">SOA TTL</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">The default TTL for records in the reverse zone, in seconds (e.g. <code>300</code>).</dd>
        </dl>

        <h6 class="mt-4">Initial Address Blocks</h6>
        <p class="mb-0">When creating a new subnet, the form includes optional inline fields to define a <strong>Reserved Block</strong> and a <strong>Fixed Block</strong> immediately — saving a trip to a separate form after saving. Enter a start IP, end IP, and optional label for each block you want to create. Leave the fields blank to skip. Additional blocks can always be added later from the subnet detail page.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="subnet-detail">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-eye me-1"></i>Subnet Detail Page</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>Click a subnet name to open its detail page. This shows:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Summary bar</strong> — VRF, gateway, VLAN, DNS views, and tags at a glance</li>
            <li><strong>DNS Records</strong> — records attached directly to the subnet (e.g. reverse-zone SOA/NS records)</li>
            <li><strong>Address Blocks</strong> — reserved or fixed IP ranges within the subnet</li>
            <li><strong>IPv4 / IPv6 Allocations</strong> — every IP address assigned to an interface in this subnet, with a link to the interface and host. Shows the next available address and count of free addresses.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="address-blocks">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-grid me-1"></i>Address Blocks</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>Address blocks let you mark ranges of IPs within a subnet as having a specific purpose. Add them from the subnet detail page using <strong>Add Block</strong>.</p>
        <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-3">
                <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td><span class="badge bg-success">Fixed</span></td>
                        <td>Addresses assignable to interfaces. <strong>Auto-assign picks exclusively from Fixed blocks</strong> when any exist on the subnet. Manual IP entry is also allowed within Fixed blocks.</td>
                        <td><code>10.1.2.100</code> – <code>10.1.2.200</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><span class="badge bg-warning text-dark">Dynamic</span></td>
                        <td>DHCP dynamic pool range (tracked for reference only). DashDDI does <strong>not</strong> use Dynamic blocks for auto-assignment or validation — they are informational and can be referenced when generating DHCP server config.</td>
                        <td><code>10.1.2.50</code> – <code>10.1.2.99</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><span class="badge bg-danger">Reserved</span></td>
                        <td>Infrastructure or excluded addresses (e.g. gateway, network appliances). Auto-assign <strong>never</strong> uses Reserved blocks. Manual IP entry <em>is</em> allowed within Reserved blocks.</td>
                        <td><code>10.1.2.1</code> – <code>10.1.2.10</code></td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <h6>How blocks affect IP assignment</h6>
        <ul class="mb-3">
            <li><strong>Auto-assign</strong> — When Fixed blocks exist on the subnet, the next available IP is drawn exclusively from Fixed blocks, in order. If no Fixed blocks are defined, auto-assign falls back to the full subnet CIDR range.</li>
            <li><strong>Manual entry</strong> — When Fixed or Reserved blocks exist on the subnet, a manually entered IP must fall within one of those blocks (either Fixed or Reserved). Dynamic blocks are ignored for this check. If no Fixed or Reserved blocks are defined, the IP just needs to be within the subnet CIDR.</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="mb-0">Each block has a start IP, end IP, and an optional label.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="vrfs">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-signpost-split me-1"></i>VRFs (Virtual Routing &amp; Forwarding)</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>A <strong>VRF</strong> is a named routing context that isolates traffic between different network segments. If your organization uses VRFs, assign subnets to them so DashDDI can group and filter address space by VRF.</p>
        <p>Manage VRFs at <strong>DHCP → VRFs</strong>. Each VRF just needs a name and an optional description. Once created, you can assign it to any subnet.</p>
        <p class="mb-0 text-muted small">VRFs in DashDDI are informational — they help organize your IPAM data but do not configure routers.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="bulk-edit">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-pencil-square me-1"></i>Bulk Edit Subnets</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>From the subnet list, click <strong>Bulk Edit</strong> to update multiple subnets at once. You can change VLAN, VRF, gateway, DNS views, tags, or DHCP server assignment in bulk without editing each subnet individually.</p>
        <p class="mb-0 text-muted small">Only fields you explicitly set in the bulk editor are updated — fields left blank are left unchanged.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="import">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-upload me-1"></i>Importing Subnets</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>DashDDI can parse an existing Kea DHCP configuration file and import subnets and host reservations from it. Go to <strong>DHCP → Subnets</strong> and click <strong>Import</strong>.</p>
        <ol class="mb-0">
            <li>Upload the Kea config file (<code>kea-dhcp4.conf</code> or similar)</li>
            <li>DashDDI parses the file and shows a preview of subnets and hosts to be created, flagging any conflicts with existing records</li>
            <li>Review the preview and confirm to import</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

</div>{# /col-lg-9 #}

<div class="col-lg-3 d-none d-lg-block">
    <div class="card sticky-top" style="top:80px">
        <div class="card-header small fw-semibold">On this page</div>
        <div class="list-group list-group-flush small">
            <a href="#overview" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">Overview</a>
            <a href="#subnet-list" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">Subnet List Views</a>
            <a href="#create-subnet" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">Creating a Subnet</a>
            <a href="#subnet-detail" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">Subnet Detail Page</a>
            <a href="#address-blocks" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">Address Blocks</a>
            <a href="#vrfs" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">VRFs</a>
            <a href="#bulk-edit" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">Bulk Edit</a>
            <a href="#import" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">Importing Subnets</a>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

</div>{# /row #}
{% endblock %}
